<h1 class="page-title">Cyberpedia</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Music &amp; sound design &middot; 2024&ndash; &middot; Bitcrush</p>

<p>Cyberpedia is a point-and-click puzzle game by Chris Erickson (Bitcrush), dressed up as a 90s educational CD-ROM: boot it up and, through the power of the internet, it builds a museum full of exhibits. Thirty years on, the links are broken, information has gone missing and a "generative" trend is spreading through the museum, so you and your assistant, Bookworth, set about putting it right.</p>
<p>I made all of its music and sound effects over a semester at RMIT in 2024, and stayed on as the game kept growing. An early demo is now on itch.io, with sound effects by me and music by Max Kearney and me, and in 2025 the game was backed by Screen Australia's Emerging Gamemakers Fund.</p>

{% video { youtube: "sNgPT3m4pEo", title: "Cyberpedia full playthrough", caption: "A full playthrough from the end of 2024, with all my music and sound." } %}

<h2>Sounding like 1996</h2>
<p>To get the sound of a 90s kids' game, I listened to a lot of them (Treasure MathStorm!, Kid Pix, the Bill Nye theme) and noticed the same things: warbly tape saturation and sampled 80s drums. So everything went through tape emulation, the drums came from a VHS-era sample pack, the synths are deliberately cheap, and it was all exported at 11 kHz and 8-bit, like the CD-ROM games it's imitating.</p>
<p>The music: two title themes (Chris liked both enough to use them in different places), a funky tutorial theme, a jingle for the made-up publisher PlaySmart Interactive, and a track for each museum stage: Ancient Greece, Egypt and the Aztecs. The stage tracks share a tempo, key and length, so the music can move smoothly from one to the next, and the HUD sounds are tuned to the same key.</p>

