<h2>Sound effects</h2>
<p>The sound effects are just as 90s: menu buttons and books on the shelf that play little musical notes, a nerdy babble for Bookworth (my own voice, chopped into syllables and pitched down, one per letter), a printer whirr when you fix an exhibit (it started out as a dial-up modem, which would have got annoying fast), a different sound when a fix fails, curtains that open and close between scenes, and some silly dinosaur noises in the tutorial.</p>
<p>Chris built the game with Adventure Creator, a visual scripting kit for Unity that handles most sounds (on click, on hover) without code. Where it didn't, I wrote C# scripts: the dialogue babble, the cursor sounds and the music changes between stages. For the final mix, Unity couldn't adjust each clip's volume, so I brought everything in peaking at &minus;6&nbsp;dB and re-bounced anything that sat wrong from Reaper.</p>

